Here is the reasoning and the structure.

## Why `unique_ptr`, not `shared_ptr`

`shared_ptr` exists for the case where ownership is genuinely shared across objects with independent lifetimes. Here it is the opposite: one creator, one owner, one lifetime. Reaching for `shared_ptr` anyway would:

- add an atomic reference count you never need,
- make it possible to accidentally extend the file's lifetime past where you intended,
- and, most importantly, obscure the plain fact that this resource has exactly one owner.

The rule of thumb: default to `unique_ptr`; a single-owner resource like this is exactly what it is for. `shared_ptr` would be a code smell here.

## The file handle: `unique_ptr<FILE, FileCloser>`

A raw `FILE*` is not owned by any standard smart pointer out of the box, so you supply a deleter. Give it a named type rather than a lambda so it can be reused and read clearly:

```cpp
// logger.hpp
#pragma once
#include <cstdio>
#include <memory>
#include <string>
#include <string_view>

struct FileCloser {
    void operator()(std::FILE* f) const noexcept {
        if (f) std::fclose(f);
    }
};

using FileHandle = std::unique_ptr<std::FILE, FileCloser>;
```

If you are on C++23 and the project targets it, `std::fopen` pairs naturally with `std::fclose`; if you prefer POSIX `open`/`close`, the same pattern applies with an `FdCloser` that calls `::close`. Match whatever the rest of the codebase already uses rather than introducing a second convention.

## The Logger class

The key structural decision: **make the Logger own the handle, be non-copyable, and be movable.** A `unique_ptr` member automatically makes the class non-copyable, which is correct — you must never be able to duplicate a Logger and have two objects trying to close the same handle.

```cpp
// logger.hpp
class Logger {
public:
    // Factory: the only way to construct one, so the "file opened?" state
    // is always valid.
    static std::unique_ptr<Logger> open(std::string_view path);

    ~Logger() = default;                 // FileHandle closes the file

    Logger(const Logger&)            = delete;
    Logger& operator=(const Logger&) = delete;
    Logger(Logger&&)                 = default;
    Logger& operator=(Logger&&)      = default;

    void log(std::string_view message);

private:
    explicit Logger(FileHandle file) : file_(std::move(file)) {}

    FileHandle file_;
    // std::mutex mutex_;   // add only if log() is actually called concurrently
};
```

```cpp
// logger.cpp
#include "logger.hpp"

std::unique_ptr<Logger> Logger::open(std::string_view path) {
    // NUL-terminate for the C API.
    std::string p(path);
    std::FILE* raw = std::fopen(p.c_str(), "a");
    if (!raw) {
        return nullptr;                 // caller checks; no half-built Logger exists
    }
    // Wrap immediately so the handle is owned from this line onward.
    return std::unique_ptr<Logger>(new Logger(FileHandle{raw}));
}

void Logger::log(std::string_view message) {
    if (!file_) return;
    std::fwrite(message.data(), 1, message.size(), file_.get());
    std::fputc('\n', file_.get());
    std::fflush(file_.get());
}
```

Points worth calling out:

- **Factory function rather than a public constructor.** Opening a file can fail. If the constructor did the open, you would either throw or have a constructor that can leave the object in an invalid state — both worse than a factory that returns `nullptr` (or throws, if the project's convention is exceptions; pick one and stay consistent with the surrounding code). The factory also guarantees every constructed Logger holds a live handle, so no method ever needs to ask "am I open?".
- **Wrap the raw pointer on the very next line after `fopen`.** Never let a `FILE*` sit unwrapped across any statement that could throw or return early — that is the one window where the handle leaks.
- **`std::unique_ptr<Logger>` returned from the factory.** The single caller stores it by value in whatever object owns it, or in a `std::unique_ptr<Logger>` member. Because it is a `unique_ptr`, ownership is explicit and cannot be accidentally copied.
- **The destructor is `= default`.** There is nothing to do: `FileHandle`'s destructor calls `FileCloser`, which calls `fclose`. This is the whole point of RAII — you do not write a destructor, so you cannot forget to close in one of several exit paths.
- **Add a mutex only if `log()` is genuinely called from multiple threads.** Do not pre-emptively add one; add it, with a `std::lock_guard` at the top of `log()`, when the concurrency requirement actually appears.

## How the single owner holds it

Wherever that one part of the program is — say a `Application` or `ServerContext` object — store the Logger as a member:

```cpp
class Application {
    // ...
    std::unique_ptr<Logger> logger_;
};
```

and initialize it once:

```cpp
