The Logger class owns the file handle for its whole lifetime, and only one part of the program creates or holds it — so the answer follows directly from `unique_ptr`'s single-owner semantics rather than from `shared_ptr`'s shared-ownership refcounting.

## Which smart pointer

**`std::unique_ptr` for the file handle**, not `shared_ptr`.

- "Only one part of the program ever creates or holds it" is the exact definition of unique ownership — `unique_ptr` encodes that in the type system, and it is non-copyable, so the compiler will *reject* any accidental copy of the Logger that would produce two owners of one file handle.
- `shared_ptr` here would buy nothing and cost something: atomic refcounting on every copy/move, and it would hide the fact that the single-owner relationship is intentional. If you ever do need a `shared_ptr<Logger>` (e.g. handing the logger to several subsystems that each keep a reference), that belongs at the *Logger* level, not at the file-handle level — the handle itself is still uniquely owned by the Logger.
