- Confirm the class is actually non-copyable: try to copy it in a scratch test and check the compiler rejects it — this verifies the ownership invariant is enforced, not just intended.
- Confirm there is exactly one owner of the `FILE*` at all times: no public accessor, no raw `FILE*` escaping a method, and the move constructor leaves the source empty.

For a `Logger` that owns one file handle for its lifetime and has exactly one creator/owner, use `std::unique_ptr` — `shared_ptr` would be the wrong choice here.

## Why unique_ptr

- Ownership is exclusive by your own description ("only one part of the program ever creates or holds it"). That is precisely the single-owner case `unique_ptr` exists for.
- `shared_ptr` would add atomic refcount traffic on every copy and, more importantly, obscure the one-owner relationship in the type itself. It is not a "safer default" — it is a statement that ownership is genuinely shared, which yours is not.
- `unique_ptr` is move-only, so the compiler enforces the single-owner invariant for you: the type system won't let the handle be copied into two Loggers.

## Structure

Hold the file handle as a `std::unique_ptr<FILE, decltype(&fclose)>` (C stdio) or `std::unique_ptr<std::ofstream>` (C++ iostreams). For C stdio you need a custom deleter because `fclose`, not `delete`, is the correct release:

```cpp
// logger.hpp
#pragma once
#include <cstdio>
#include <memory>
#include <string_view>

class Logger {
public:
    // Sole factory: performs the open, returns nullptr-free unique_ptr on success.
    static std::unique_ptr<Logger> Open(const char* path);

    // Move-only. Delete copy so the single-owner invariant is enforced.
    Logger(Logger&&) noexcept = default;
    Logger& operator=(Logger&&) noexcept = default;
    Logger(const Logger&) = delete;
    Logger& operator=(const Logger&) = delete;

    ~Logger() = default;  // unique_ptr deleter closes the file

    void Log(std::string_view message);

private:
    // fclose is the correct release; decltype gives the deleter type.
    using FileHandle = std::unique_ptr<std::FILE, decltype(&std::fclose)>;

    explicit Logger(FileHandle file) : file_(std::move(file)) {}

    FileHandle file_;
};
```

```cpp
// logger.cpp
#include "logger.hpp"

std::unique_ptr<Logger> Logger::Open(const char* path) {
    std::FILE* raw = std::fopen(path, "a");
    if (raw == nullptr) {
        return nullptr;  // or throw / std::expected, per project convention
    }
    // Wrap immediately; from here on fclose is guaranteed on every exit path.
    return std::unique_ptr<Logger>(new Logger(FileHandle{raw, &std::fclose}));
}
```

## Key points

- **Wrap the raw handle immediately after `fopen` succeeds.** The moment you have a non-null `FILE*` wrapped in the `unique_ptr`, the destructor runs `fclose` on every exit path — early returns, exceptions, everything. No manual `fclose` to forget.
- **`fclose`, not `delete`.** `FILE*` came from `fopen`, so `delete` would be undefined behavior. The custom deleter is what makes `unique_ptr` usable for C-style resources.
- **Move-only.** Deleting the copy operations encodes "single owner" in the type. If some other code needs to pass the Logger around without owning it, pass `Logger*` or `Logger&`, not a second `unique_ptr`.
- **Return by `unique_ptr`, not by value.** Loggers own a resource and have identity; the creator receives ownership explicitly. If you can use C++17 guaranteed copy elision to return the object by value that's fine too, but returning `unique_ptr` makes the ownership transfer visible at the call site.
- **Prefer `std::ofstream` if the project already uses iostreams** — then no custom deleter is needed and the class is even simpler. Pick whichever matches the surrounding codebase's existing I/O style; don't mix `FILE*` and streams for the same resource.

## One caveat worth flagging

The single-owner design is correct only if your "only one part of the program ever holds it" claim holds for the *whole* lifetime. If a second subsystem later needs to write to the same log concurrently, do not reflexively switch to `shared_ptr<Logger>` — that introduces two owners and needs synchronization on the shared `FILE*` anyway. Better to keep one owning `Logger` and hand out a non-owning `Logger&`/`Logger*` to the other subsystem, with a mutex inside `Logger` guarding `Log()`. `shared_ptr` buys you lifetime, not thread safety.

