The right way is to make the worker's completion observable and wait on that, not on wall-clock time:

```cpp
TEST_F(QueueProcessorTest, ProcessesAllItems) {
    QueueProcessor processor;
    std::thread worker([&] { processor.RunUntilDrained(); });

    // ... enqueue / drive input ...

    worker.join();          // blocks until the thread function returns
    EXPECT_TRUE(processor.QueueEmpty());
    EXPECT_EQ(processor.ProcessedCount(), kExpectedItems);
}
```

`join()` is the primary tool: it blocks until the thread's function returns and guarantees a happens-before edge between everything the thread did and everything the test reads afterward. That edge is also what makes the subsequent reads of the queue's state (count, contents, flags) well-defined under the C++ memory model and what keeps ThreadSanitizer quiet about them — reading the queue without joining first would be a data race even if you "knew" the worker had finished.

A few things to get right beyond the bare join:

**Don't `sleep` as the synchronization.** `std::this_thread::sleep_for(100ms)` is the classic version of this bug: it makes the test slower on the happy path and flaky the moment the machine is loaded or the queue is larger than usual. `join()` is the only thing that actually proves the work finished.

**If the worker runs indefinitely (a consumer loop waiting for more work), joining won't return on its own.** Add an explicit stop signal and join that:

```cpp
processor.RequestStop();   // sets an atomic flag, notifies a condvar
worker.join();
```

A `std::condition_variable` with a predicate is the usual mechanism:

```cpp
std::mutex m;
std::condition_variable cv;
bool done = false;

std::thread worker([&] {
    processor.Run();
    {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lk(m);
        done = true;
    }
    cv.notify_one();
});

{
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lk(m);
    cv.wait(lk, [&] { return done; });
}
worker.join();   // still join to release the thread's resources
```

Here the `cv.wait` predicate handles the spurious-wakeup case for you, and the join is still required afterward to reclaim the thread.

**If you want the main thread doing something while waiting rather than blocking, use a `std::future`** and call `.wait()` or `.get()`:

```cpp
auto fut = std::async(std::launch::async, [&] { processor.RunUntilDrained(); });
// ... other work ...
fut.get();   // blocks until the task completes, rethrows anything it threw
```

`.get()` gives you the completion wait and propagates an exception out of the worker, which a raw `join()` would silently swallow.

**Keep the join unconditional.** If an `ASSERT_*` fires partway through the test body, GoogleTest `return`s out of the test function — so a `join()` placed only at the end is skipped, and the still-running thread outlives the fixture's teardown. Either do the join before any assertion that could abort the test, or use RAII so destruction always joins:

```cpp
class JThread {
public:
    explicit JThread(std::function<void()> f) : t_(std::move(f)) {}
    ~JThread() { if (t_.joinable()) t_.join(); }
    JThread(const JThread&) = delete;
    JThread& operator=(const JThread&) = delete;
private:
    std::thread t_;
};
```

(`std::jthread` does exactly this — auto-join on destruction — if you're on C++20.)

**Then assert on the final state after the join**, as in the first snippet. Since the join established the happens-before edge, reading `processor.QueueEmpty()`, `ProcessedCount()`, or the queue's contents in the test thread is safe and sees every write the worker made.

One caution specific to your setup: a GoogleTest death test defaults to `focus`/`fast` style, which forks the test process. Forking while a background thread is live is undefined — only the forking thread survives in the child. If any case in this fixture is a death test, set `GTEST_FLAG_SET(death_test_style, "threadsafe")` for it (or globally), or make sure the worker thread is joined before the death assertion runs.

Finally, run this fixture at least once under ThreadSanitizer:

```bash
cmake -S . -B build-tsan -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug \
  -DCMAKE_CXX_FLAGS='-fsanitize=thread -fno-omit-frame-pointer -g'
cmake --build build-tsan && ctest --test-dir build-tsan --output-on-failure
```

TSan will flag any shared queue state the test reads or the worker writes without the join/cv edge in place, which is the exact class of mistake a `sleep`-based wait tends to hide.

The right way is to **join the thread** before you assert — or, if you don't want to block the test thread, wait on a condition variable or a future that the worker sets when it's done. Never `sleep` for "long enough."

## The pattern

**1. Join the thread (simplest, and usually correct for a fixture):**

```cpp
TEST_F(QueueProcessorTest, DrainsAllItems) {
  auto worker = std::thread([this] { processor_.ProcessUntilDrained(); });

  for (int i = 0; i < 100; ++i) {
    queue_.Push(Item{i});
  }
  queue_.Close();            // signal the worker it can stop

  worker.join();             // <-- wait here, deterministically

  EXPECT_TRUE(queue_.Empty());
  EXPECT_EQ(processor_.ProcessedCount(), 100);
}
```

`join()` returns only once the worker's lambda has actually finished, so every read after it is properly sequenced — no data race on `queue_`'s state, and no arbitrary timeout to tune.

**2. Condition variable, when the test needs to observe an intermediate state rather than just the end:**

```cpp
TEST_F(QueueProcessorTest, SignalsDrainCompletion) {
  std::mutex m;
  std::condition_variable cv;
  bool done = false;

  auto worker = std::thread([&] {
    processor_.ProcessUntilDrained();
    {
      std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lk(m);
      done = true;
    }
    cv.notify_one();
  });

  // ... push work, close the queue ...
  worker.join();

  std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lk(m);
  cv.wait(lk, [&] { return done; });   // still have join, plus a real predicate wait
  EXPECT_TRUE(queue_.Empty());
}
```

**3. `std::future`/`std::promise`, if the test also wants the worker's result:**

```cpp
std::promise<size_t> p;
auto fut = p.get_future();
std::thread worker([&] {
  p.set_value(processor_.ProcessUntilDrained());
});

// ... push work, close the queue ...
EXPECT_EQ(fut.get(), 100);   // blocks until the worker resolves the promise
worker.join();
```

## Why not `sleep`

`sleep_for(100ms)` "passes" only when the machine happens to be fast enough that run — it's a coin flip under CI load, and the failure mode is exactly the flaky test everyone hates debugging. Joining the thread (or waiting on the predicate) makes the ordering a guarantee, not a bet.

## Related gotchas for this specific shape of test

- **Give the worker a deterministic exit condition.** `ProcessUntilDrained()` needs a "close"/sentinel so it isn't racing an empty queue. If your worker polls the queue forever, `join()` will deadlock — close the queue first, then join.
- **Don't push work after closing** unless the producer is also a joined thread; interleave producer and consumer threads explicitly and join both.
- **Fixture teardown must join too.** If a `TEST_F` fixture owns the worker thread, join it in `TearDown()` so a failing `ASSERT_*` mid-test doesn't strand a live thread (`std::thread`'s destructor calls `std::terminate` on a joinable thread, which turns an assertion failure into a crash).
- **Death tests:** if any test in the binary is a death test and the binary is multi-threaded, set `GTEST_FLAG_SET(death_test_style, "threadsafe")` — the default `fast` style forks a process with more than one live thread and can misbehave.
- **Run the suite under ThreadSanitizer at least once.** Join/condition-variable correctness is exactly what TSan verifies: it catches any remaining unguarded shared access between the worker and the test thread, including ones a join alone wouldn't prove absent (e.g., the worker still touching shared state after the test read it, or a second un-joined thread). Build TSan as a separate configuration from ASan/UBSan — the runtimes can't be linked into one binary.

The right way is to **join the thread** (or wait on a condition variable/future) — never `sleep`. A `sleep_for(100ms)` is a guess about scheduling, not a guarantee; under a loaded CI machine or a debug build it will flake. A plain `join()` is both a synchronization point and, as a side effect, a memory-ordering barrier: once the thread that wrote to the queue has been joined, the joining thread is guaranteed to observe all of that thread's writes. That is exactly what you need before asserting on the queue's final state.

**Basic shape:**

```cpp
TEST_F(QueueProcessorTest, DrainsAllItems) {
  std::thread worker([this] { processor_.Run(); });

  for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
    queue_.Push(i);
  }
  queue_.Shutdown();  // signals Run() to exit its loop

  worker.join();      // <-- synchronization point; safe to read queue_ now

  EXPECT_TRUE(queue_.Empty());
  EXPECT_EQ(processed_.size(), 5u);
}
```

A few things that make this robust rather than merely passing today:

**1. The worker loop needs a deterministic stop condition.** `join()` only helps if the thread *will* terminate. Have the worker block on a condition variable and exit when a `done_`/`shutdown_` flag is set, rather than polling the queue:

```cpp
void Processor::Run() {
  std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mu_);
  cv_.wait(lock, [this] { return shutdown_ || !queue_.empty(); });
  while (!shutdown_) {
    // pop, process, then re-wait
    cv_.wait(lock, [this] { return shutdown_ || !queue_.empty(); });
  }
}
```

That way `Shutdown()` sets the flag and calls `cv_.notify_all()`, and `Run()` returns promptly — no timing assumption anywhere.

**2. If you can't restructure the loop, wait on the same primitive the producer uses.** A `std::future`/`std::promise`, a latch, or a condition variable that the worker signals when it's finished all work. Join the thread after the signal, or just wait on the signal if the worker's own exit is what you're confirming.

**3. Prefer RAII so a failing assertion can't leak a live thread.** If an `EXPECT_*` before `join()` were to return early in some refactor, or an exception unwinds, a still-joinable `std::thread` destructor calls `std::terminate()`. Wrap the thread so it always joins:

```cpp
struct Joiner {
  std::thread& t;
  ~Joiner() { if (t.joinable()) t.join(); }
};
```

or hold it in a small RAII thread wrapper. Then `join()` explicitly before the assertions, and the destructor is a harmless no-op afterward.

**4. Run the whole test under TSan at least once.** You're asserting on state written by one thread and read by another; a missing lock or a non-atomic flag inside the worker is a data race that join ordering alone won't reveal, and ThreadSanitizer is the tool that catches it at low cost. Build a separate configuration — never link TSan together with ASan/UBSan:

```bash
cmake -S . -B build-tsan -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug \
  -DCMAKE_CXX_FLAGS='-fsanitize=thread -fno-omit-frame-pointer -g'
cmake --build build-tsan && ctest --test-dir build-tsan --output-on-failure
```

**What not to do:** `sleep_for`, `usleep`, or polling a flag in a spin loop with a timeout. Each replaces a real happens-before edge with a race against the scheduler — the test passes on your laptop and fails in CI, which is the worst kind of test.

So: push your inputs, signal shutdown, `join()` (with RAII as the safety net), then assert — and verify once under TSan.

Join the thread. Keep the `std::thread` (or `std::jthread` if the project is on C++20) as a member of the fixture, start it in `SetUp()`/the test body, and call `.join()` before the assertions:

```cpp
class QueueProcessorTest : public ::testing::Test {
 protected:
  void SetUp() override {
    worker_ = std::thread([this] { processor_.run(); });
  }
  void TearDown() override {
    if (worker_.joinable()) worker_.join();  // safety net for a failing test body
  }

  QueueProcessor processor_;
  std::thread worker_;
};

TEST_F(QueueProcessorTest, DrainsQueueToEmpty) {
  processor_.push(1);
  processor_.push(2);
  processor_.stop();       // signal the worker to finish and return
  worker_.join();          // wait for it to actually be done

  EXPECT_TRUE(processor_.queue().empty());
  EXPECT_EQ(processor_.processed(), 2);
}
```

The important part: `join()` only returns once the worker's thread function has fully returned (`run()` has exited), so every write it made to the queue is ordered-before your assertions. That's the guarantee you need — nothing about `sleep_for` or a timer gives you that.

A few things that matter here:

**Never synchronize with `sleep_for` / `usleep`.** A sleep of "long enough" is a bet on scheduling that loses under CI load or a debug build, and produces a flaky failure that looks like a product bug. If the worker blocks waiting for work, use a condition variable or a stop flag so you can signal it deterministically, then join.

**If the worker needs to be asked to stop**, give it a real stop mechanism (`stop()`, a CV predicate, `std::stop_token` on C++20) rather than relying on the queue being empty at some instant — otherwise `join()` can hang forever. Set up `TearDown()` to join if still joinable so a failing assertion in the body doesn't leave a detached thread running into the next test.

**If the queue is shared across more than one thread**, run the suite under TSan as well, since `join()` orders *this* thread's view but won't catch an unsynchronized access between two workers:

```bash
cmake -S . -B build-tsan -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug \
  -DCMAKE_CXX_FLAGS='-fsanitize=thread -fno-omit-frame-pointer -g'
cmake --build build-tsan && ctest --test-dir build-tsan --output-on-failure
```

**Use `EXPECT_*` for the final-state checks** (as above) so a mismatch in `queue().empty()` doesn't suppress the `processed()` check — reserve `ASSERT_*` for the case where a null/bad result means later lines can't run safely.

One caveat on the fixture: if `join()` can block for a real duration, prefer starting the worker in the test body rather than `SetUp()` so the wait is scoped to the test that actually needs it, and keep exactly one join — a second `join()` on the same thread is a `std::system_error`.

The right way is to join the thread (or wait on a condition variable / future) — never `sleep`. Concretely, options in order of preference:

**1. Store the thread in the fixture and join it in the test body (or `TearDown()`):**

```cpp
class QueueWorkerTest : public ::testing::Test {
protected:
  void SetUp() override {
    worker_ = std::thread([this] { worker_.Run(&queue_); });
  }
  void TearDown() override {
    queue_.Shutdown();      // let the worker observe it should stop
    if (worker_.joinable()) worker_.join();
  }
  BlockingQueue queue_;     // or whatever the real type is
  Worker worker_;
};

TEST_F(QueueWorkerTest, DrainsAllItemsAndReportsFinalState) {
  queue_.Push(1);
  queue_.Push(2);
  queue_.Push(3);
  queue_.Shutdown();        // worker exits once the queue is closed and drained
  worker_.join();           // <-- synchronizes: happens-after the worker's last write
  EXPECT_EQ(queue_.Size(), 0u);
  EXPECT_EQ(worker_.ProcessedCount(), 3u);
}
```

Join is the key part: it establishes a happens-before relationship, so the main thread is guaranteed to see every write the worker made. That's what makes asserting on the "final state" safe — a `sleep` gives you no such guarantee, and worse, the test flakes under load.

**2. If the worker signals completion rather than being joinable from the test, use a future or condition variable:**

```cpp
std::promise<void> done;
std::future<void> finished = done.get_future();
std::thread worker([&] {
  ProcessAll(&queue_);
  done.set_value();
});

// ... push work, then:
finished.wait();          // or finished.get()
worker.join();

EXPECT_EQ(queue_.Size(), 0u);
```

Or a `std::condition_variable` with a predicate, if the worker needs to signal progress rather than just "all done":

```cpp
std::mutex m;
std::condition_variable cv;
bool drained = false;

// worker, when the queue is empty and closed:
{ std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lk(m); drained = true; }
cv.notify_one();

// test:
{
  std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lk(m);
  cv.wait(lk, [&] { return drained; });
}
worker.join();
// assert on queue_ here
```

**Things to avoid / watch for:**

- **No `sleep_for`, `usleep`, or polling loops.** They don't synchronize; they just make the test slow and flaky.
- **Join inside `TearDown()`, not the destructor.** If setup can fail and the test asserts on it, `TearDown` is the reliable place; and GoogleTest runs `TearDown` after the test body regardless of assertion failures, so the worker is always cleaned up.
- **Join before you assert**, not after — and if the worker thread could still be touching `queue_`, the expectation must come after the join, exactly as above.
- **Make sure the worker actually terminates.** If it blocks forever on an empty queue, join hangs; the shutdown/close signal (or a "stop" flag + `notify_all`) is what lets it exit cleanly.
