Concretely, three patterns, in order of preference:

**1. Join the thread (simplest, if the worker exits after draining the queue):**

```cpp
TEST_F(QueueTest, ProcessesAllItems) {
  std::thread worker([this] { drain(queue); });   // returns when queue is empty
  worker.join();                                   // full memory barrier + happens-before
  EXPECT_TRUE(queue.empty());
  EXPECT_EQ(processed.size(), kNumItems);
}
```

`join()` gives you both the synchronization you need (everything the worker wrote is visible to the test thread afterward) and a guaranteed return point — no polling, no timeout.

**2. Signal completion through a condition variable (if the worker stays alive):**

```cpp
TEST_F(QueueTest, ProcessesAllItems) {
  std::thread worker([this] { run_until_stopped(); });

  queue.push(item1);
  queue.push(item2);
  {
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lk(m);
