The right way is to join the thread, or wait on a condition variable / future that the worker signals — never a sleep.

Since your worker is draining a queue, there are two clean patterns, depending on whether you keep the `std::thread` handle or wrap the work in a future.

**Pattern 1: join the thread (simplest)**

If your fixture owns the thread:

```cpp
#include <gtest/gtest.h>
#include <thread>

class QueueDrainTest : public ::testing::Test {
protected:
  void SetUp() override {
    worker_ = std::thread([this] { queue_.Drain(); });
  }

  void TearDown() override {
    if (worker_.joinable()) worker_.join();
  }

  Queue queue_;      // your producer/consumer queue under test
  std::thread worker_;
};

TEST_F(QueueDrainTest, DrainsAllEnqueuedItems) {
  queue_.Push(1);
  queue_.Push(2);
  queue_.Push(3);
  queue_.Close();                 // signal "no more work" — see note below

  worker_.join();                 // deterministic: the worker has finished

  ASSERT_TRUE(worker_.joinable() == false);
  EXPECT_EQ(queue_.Size(), 0);
  EXPECT_EQ(queue_.ProcessedCount(), 3);
}
```

**Pattern 2: use `std::async` / a future**

You get the join for free in the future's destructor, and it fits a fixture less tightly:

```cpp
TEST_F(QueueDrainTest, DrainsAllEnqueuedItems) {
  queue_.Push(1);
  queue_.Push(2);
  queue_.Push(3);
  queue_.Close();

  auto done = std::async(std::launch::async, [this] { queue_.Drain(); });

  done.get();                     // blocks until Drain() returns and rethrows any exception

  EXPECT_EQ(queue_.Size(), 0);
  EXPECT_EQ(queue_.ProcessedCount(), 3);
}
```

If you don't want the fixture to own the thread, `done.get()` (or `worker_.join()`) placed immediately before the assertions is the whole answer — the assertions then run with the worker provably stopped.

**Important: the worker has to be able to finish**

A join only terminates if the worker's loop actually exits. A queue drain loop usually blocks on an empty queue, so a bare `join()` deadlocks. Close the queue (or push a sentinel, or set an atomic `stop_` flag) so `Drain()` sees "no more work" and returns. In the example above, `queue_.Close()` does that before the join.

**If you want to assert on an intermediate state while the worker is still running**, that's the one case where a join is the wrong tool. Then use a condition variable the worker signals, and wait on it under a mutex — not a sleep:

```cpp
std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lk(mu_);
cv_.wait(lk, [this] { return queue_.Size() >= 3; });
```

The worker calls `cv_.notify_one()` (with the mutex held) after each item. That gives you a deterministic wait for "at least three items are in" without ever racing a timer.

**Why not `sleep_for`:** a sleep is a guess about how long the worker needs. Under CI load or an ASan/TSan build (which runs the binary several times slower), the guess is wrong — either the test flakes because the worker isn't done, or it passes for the wrong reason because the assertion happened to run after the worker finished. Joining (or waiting on a CV/future) makes the ordering a hard guarantee rather than a timing hope.

**One more thing for concurrency tests:** if the worker shares any state with the asserting thread (the queue's size/counters, a `stop_` flag, etc.), run this test under ThreadSanitizer at least once:

```bash
cmake -S . -B build-tsan -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug \
  -DCMAKE_CXX_FLAGS='-fsanitize=thread -fno-omit-frame-pointer -g'
cmake --build build-tsan && ctest --test-dir build-tsan --output-on-failure
```

Joining the thread removes the *test's* race on the final assertion, but it will not hide a race inside the queue itself — and TSan is the tool that catches that. Build ASan/UBSan and TSan as separate configurations; they can't be linked into the same binary.

